import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as d}from"./chunks/framework.yZB1U4Nl.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Observability","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"observability.md","filePath":"observability.md"}'),s={name:"observability.md"};function n(r,e,i,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="observability" tabindex="-1">Observability <a class="header-anchor" href="#observability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Observability&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Vlo has no separate tracing store. The journal is the trace: every durable fact about a run is one row in <code>events</code>, and the two things that are not durable — <code>cmd.emit</code> payloads and dispatcher warnings — go over an in-memory side channel. Source: <code>src/host/**</code>.</p><h2 id="the-journal-is-the-trace" tabindex="-1">The journal is the trace <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-journal-is-the-trace" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The journal is the trace&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each event carries enough to reconstruct what happened and why:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>seq</code></td><td>Position in the run. Strictly increasing, assigned by the kernel under the run lock.</td></tr><tr><td><code>kind</code> / <code>status</code></td><td>Which step type moved (<code>task</code>, <code>timer</code>, <code>signal</code>, <code>child</code>, <code>marker</code>) and to which of the seven statuses.</td></tr><tr><td><code>step_id</code> / <code>attempt</code></td><td>Which step, which attempt. Task ids are <code>&lt;run_id&gt;:&lt;step_id&gt;</code>, idempotency keys <code>&lt;run_id&gt;:&lt;step_id&gt;:&lt;attempt&gt;</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>cause</code></td><td>Why an attempt or run stopped: <code>timeout</code>, <code>heartbeat_lost</code>, <code>app_error</code>, <code>user_cancel</code>, <code>schema_violation</code>, <code>nondeterminism_detected</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>error</code></td><td>Free text from the worker or reducer. Never interpreted by the kernel.</td></tr><tr><td><code>usage</code></td><td>What this attempt spent (<code>cpu_ms</code>, <code>tokens</code>, <code>cost_micro_usd</code>). Present only when the worker reported any.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ts_ms</code> / <code>random</code></td><td>The clock and entropy the transition saw. A replay sees the same values.</td></tr><tr><td><code>state_hash</code></td><td>Hash of the state after this transition. Every event in one transition carries the same hash.</td></tr><tr><td><code>schema_ref</code></td><td><code>name@&lt;content hash&gt;</code> of the activity or signal schema in force.</td></tr><tr><td><code>parent_run_id</code> / <code>parent_step_id</code></td><td>Set on every event of a child run from birth, so a run tree is a join.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Read it with <code>GET /v1/runs/{id}/events</code>, <code>client.events(runId, since)</code>, or straight from the table. Lifecycle events (<code>scheduled</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>retrying</code>, <code>cancelled</code>) sit next to the reducer-facing ones, so the timeline includes dispatch, lease and retry decisions, not just results.</p><p>Markers are the run-level punctuation: <code>start</code> (the input), <code>complete</code> (the output), <code>continue</code> (the next input), <code>cancel</code>, <code>fail</code> (a reducer throw; its payload is the event that was being folded, since that event&#39;s own transaction rolled back) and <code>nondeterminism</code>.</p><h2 id="usage-accounting" tabindex="-1">Usage accounting <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-accounting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage accounting&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Usage travels one way and is banked once:</p><ol><li>A handler calls <code>ctx.reportUsage({ tokens?, costMicroUsd?, cpuMs? })</code> as often as it likes; the worker tallies per attempt (integers, never negative).</li><li>The tally rides on the attempt&#39;s result — the <code>complete</code> or <code>fail</code> body over HTTP, the reporter call in-process — and lands as <code>usage</code> on the <code>task/succeeded</code>, <code>task/failed</code> or <code>task/retrying</code> event.</li><li>The kernel adds it to <code>kernel_state.usage_total</code> while folding, on lifecycle events too, so a retried attempt&#39;s spend is not lost.</li><li>Every reducer event carries <code>usage_total</code> (the banked total including this event); <code>task_done</code> also carries the step&#39;s own <code>usage</code>, zeroed when nothing was reported.</li></ol><p>A reducer can therefore decide on a budget without keeping its own tally; that is how the agent layer parks a run on a top-up signal. The current total is also readable off the checkpoint: <code>run_state.kernel_state</code> is JSON with a <code>usage_total</code> field. <code>cost_micro_usd</code> is whatever the worker reports; the engine has no price table, and child runs are not rolled up into their parent.</p><h2 id="the-side-channel" tabindex="-1">The side channel <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-side-channel" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The side channel&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>host.events</code> is a Node <code>EventEmitter</code>. Nothing on it is journaled or replayed.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Payload</th><th>Emitted when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>emit</code></td><td><code>{ run_id, channel, data }</code></td><td>A transition committed with <code>cmd.emit(channel, data)</code> commands. Also relayed on the SSE stream as <code>event: emit</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>warning</code></td><td><code>{ message, error? }</code></td><td>The dispatcher could not start, push, fire, reap or notify something, or a poll accept failed. Never thrown, never printed; subscribe if you want a log.</td></tr><tr><td><code>task_scheduled</code></td><td><code>{ run_id, task_id, queue }</code></td><td>A task row was committed. Wakes an open <code>POST /v1/tasks/poll</code> for that queue.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="language-ts vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ts</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">host.events.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">on</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;warning&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, ({ </span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">message</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">error</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> }) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> console.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">warn</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;vlo&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, message, error));</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A closed journal during shutdown is not reported as a warning; the dispatcher drops it.</p><h2 id="following-a-run-live" tabindex="-1">Following a run live <a class="header-anchor" href="#following-a-run-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Following a run live&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>client.stream(runId, { since?, signal? })</code> yields <code>journal</code>, <code>emit</code> and finally <code>end</code> messages over the SSE form of the events route (see <a href="./http-api">HTTP API</a>). Journal frames are a replay plus a tail, so reconnecting with the last <code>seq</code> loses nothing durable; emits between connections are gone. <code>client.waitFor(runId, status)</code> is the polling alternative for scripts and tests.</p><h2 id="health-and-the-outbox" tabindex="-1">Health and the outbox <a class="header-anchor" href="#health-and-the-outbox" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Health and the outbox&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>GET /healthz</code> answers <code>{ ok, roles, workflows }</code> with no auth; it says the process is up and what it serves, nothing about the journal.</li><li><code>POST /v1/timers/tick</code> answers <code>TickCounts</code> — <code>started</code>, <code>pushed</code>, <code>timers</code>, <code>reaped</code>, <code>timeouts</code>, <code>notified</code> — for one dispatcher pass, and <code>?drain=1</code> answers <code>{ passes, idle, counts }</code>. A cron that logs these has a per-minute picture of how much the outbox moved and whether it ran dry (<code>idle: true</code>) or hit its time box.</li><li><code>vlo doctor --profile &lt;name&gt; [--json]</code> runs real round trips: journal connect, migrate and commit; blob put/get; kernel wasm size and protocol; schema registration; each push queue&#39;s <code>/healthz</code>; roles against dispatcher mode. It exits 1 on any failing check. The hint strings are listed in <a href="./errors">errors</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="nondeterminism-as-a-correctness-signal" tabindex="-1">Nondeterminism as a correctness signal <a class="header-anchor" href="#nondeterminism-as-a-correctness-signal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Nondeterminism as a correctness signal&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On every fold the host re-hashes the stored checkpoint and compares it with the stored <code>state_hash</code>; when the checkpoint is missing or was written by another bundle it folds the journal from <code>seq 0</code> and compares each transition&#39;s hash with the hash journaled on the trigger event. The first disagreement ends the run: a <code>nondeterminism</code> marker with <code>cause: nondeterminism_detected</code> and the divergent <code>seq</code> in <code>error</code>, and every open task is cancelled. Treat it as a bug report, not a flake — the reducer no longer reproduces the history it wrote (a <code>Date</code>, <code>Math.random</code>, or a state shape change without <code>continue_as_new</code>). It is never retried.</p><h2 id="not-built-yet" tabindex="-1">Not built yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-built-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not built yet&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Everything below would be an additive reader over the journal; none of it needs a kernel or schema change.</p><ul><li><strong>OpenTelemetry export.</strong> A run maps to a trace and a step to a span: <code>started_at_ms</code> / <code>closed_at_ms</code> bound the trace, <code>scheduled</code> → terminal events bound each span, <code>cause</code> and <code>error</code> become span status, <code>usage</code> becomes attributes, <code>parent_run_id</code> links traces.</li><li><strong>A console.</strong> Run tree, inbox of <code>awaiting_signal</code> runs, event timeline, rewind and fork. Today the inbox is <code>GET /v1/runs?status=awaiting_signal</code>.</li><li><strong><code>assignee</code> search attribute.</strong> The <code>runs.assignee</code> column exists and is not written or queried yet.</li><li><strong>Child usage roll-up.</strong> <code>usage_total</code> is per run; a parent does not see what its children spent.</li><li><strong>Pending-migration and schema-drift detection in <code>vlo doctor</code>.</strong> It reports schemas that are absent, not ones that differ.</li></ul>',25)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
